// ESLint config, added 2026-10-20. `eslint` and `eslint-config-next` had been
// devDependencies with a `lint` script since the repo was set up, but no config
// file existed — so `next lint` dropped into its interactive setup prompt and
// `next build` linted nothing. Nothing was ever checked.
//
// `next build` fails on errors and passes on warnings, so this file is now a
// gate in the same sense next.config.js's `ignoreBuildErrors: false` is. It
// starts at 0 errors.
//
// (ESLint strips comments from .eslintrc.json, so this is legal.)
{
  "extends": "next/core-web-vitals",
  "rules": {
    // OFF, and not as a backlog deferral — the rule's advice is wrong for this
    // app. All 19 sites are images on hosts we do not control: artwork from the
    // Cleveland Museum of Art, cover art from Deezer, posters from TMDB, plus
    // signed Supabase storage URLs. next/image only accepts hosts enumerated in
    // next.config.js `images.remotePatterns`, so adopting it would mean a
    // pattern per provider and a build change every time one is added.
    //
    // It is also a measured hazard rather than a hypothetical one: the Art
    // Institute of Chicago was dropped as an artwork provider precisely because
    // its image host bot-walls hotlinked requests. Routing provider images
    // through an optimizer is the kind of change that breaks them silently.
    //
    // Re-enable for any image we host ourselves and can describe.
    "@next/next/no-img-element": "off"

    // DELIBERATELY still "warn" (47 of them): react-hooks/exhaustive-deps.
    //
    // Not silenced, because the rule finds real bugs and this codebase has the
    // exact shape it catches — effects that read refs and callbacks captured
    // from a parent. But it must NOT be mass-fixed: adding a missing dep to an
    // effect that writes state derived from that dep is how you get a render
    // loop, and several of these omissions are load-bearing (BookReader's
    // registration effects use `[]` on purpose so they register once).
    //
    // Work them one at a time, each with a reason, in a session with a browser.
  }
}
